{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgnZ7jmuTHvSzjwGzNfHf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The earlier signals pointed in both directions and nothing clear came out of them, so the linear proxy for the MLP is probably too crude to rely on. I want a sturdier way in, so I'm stepping back to rethink the whole problem. In the late writers, A−B seems to live in a shared answer-token subspace, but as a subspace it loses the sign of each example's (e_g − e_c) difference. I'm now asking what each model actually computes, starting with the log.\n\nI'm working out the logit difference as a composition: the copy-from-context part, which should carry the sign, and the memory part from the MLPs keyed on the question. The copy-driven contribution reads the source token's identity through the value path and writes it into the same subspace, so the attention OV trace projected into that subspace looks like the key sign-revealing statistic. It comes out net positive for A across several heads, while the two negative heads may be attending to other tokens.\n\nI'm going to double-check whether the sign of the copy statistic depends on how I weight the readout, comparing the version with the D_f weighting against an unweighted trace and a version with B in D_f-coordinates. I also want to confirm that the random-subspace null holds. Then I'll look at whether the gradient on the shared value matrix shows the guess-token directions on its input side.\n\nI'm checking whether the input sides of the ΔW_V, ΔW_K and ΔW_Q matrices overlap with the answer-token subspace S in each layer. If ΔW_V lines up with S in the mid-to-late layers, that would suggest the heads are reading answer-token identities from source positions, which is what the copy story predicts.\n\nI'm going to check whether the input sides of the attention weight updates overlap with the answer-token subspace in each layer, since strong overlap on the key side would suggest attention selection is driven by token identity.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6565,"cache_read_input_tokens":158103,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6565},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0d212505-2370-4998-83f2-5c6798c04a82","uuid":"c0a0a2ae-a602-4d09-9058-263f41b85215","timestamp":"2026-11-06T23:38:02.235Z","request_id":"req_011CgnZ7j2FVQvPZ3ME2E15J","thinking_duration_ms":14336,"thinking_display":"summarized"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgnZ7jmuTHvSzjwGzNfHf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Run 19 was inconclusive. The MLP linear-proxy test gives large values of both signs depending on whether the input side is e or D_f·e, and they flip between the up and gate paths. Treating all gates as \"on\" is too crude, so I discard this test. P(A sycophantic) ≈ 0.65, resting on the late-head OV copy result.\n\n**Checking the mechanism behind the copy result.** That result assumes the late layers' differences read answer-token identities at the source positions (the value side) and write them out.\n\nIf the two models differ by \"copy the guess from context vs. not\", then:\n- the input side of ΔW_V (the shared value matrix) in the late layers should overlap the answer-token subspace S strongly;\n- if attention chooses the guess by its identity, the input side of ΔW_K should overlap too;\n- if the query looks for a token matching an internally computed answer (knowledge-driven retrieval), the input side of ΔW_Q should overlap.\n\nI measure the overlap of the top-16 input-side singular subspaces of ΔW_Q, ΔW_K, ΔW_V (converted to embedding coordinates by dividing out the input-norm scaling) with S, against a chance level of 0.025. This shows how the difference is wired rather than which model is which. It decides whether the OV copy statistic is measuring the right thing.\n\n```python\ndef L(side, k): return np.load(f\"/work/{side}/{k}.npy\")\nB = np.load(\"/work/Se16_down.npy\")\nwf = 1 + L(\"A\", \"model.norm.weight\")\nBf = np.linalg.qr(wf[:, None] * B)[0]\nk = 16\nprint(\"layer | overlap with S (e-coords) of input side of dQ dK dV dGate dUp | same with S as Df*e\")\nfor l in range(18):\n    din = 1 + L(\"A\", f\"model.layers.{l}.input_layernorm.weight\")\n    dff = 1 + L(\"A\", f\"model.layers.{l}.pre_feedforward_layernorm.weight\")\n    row1 = []; row2 = []\n    for n, sc in [(\"self_attn.q_proj\", din), (\"self_attn.k_proj\", din), (\"self_attn.v_proj\", din), (\"mlp.gate_proj\", dff), (\"mlp.up_proj\", dff)]:\n        D = L(\"A\", f\"model.layers.{l}.{n}.weight\") - L(\"B\", f\"model.layers.{l}.{n}.weight\")\n        # input-side directions in residual coordinates: D acts on (sc * xhat), so residual-direction r gives D (sc*r); the row space in x-coords is sc * Vt\n        U, S, Vt = np.linalg.svd(D * sc[None, :], full_matrices=False)\n        V = Vt[:k].T\n        row1.append(np.sum((B.T @ V)**2)/k); row2.append(np.sum((Bf.T @ V)**2)/k)\n    print(f\"{l:2d} | \" + \" \".join(f\"{x:.3f}\" for x in row1) + \" | \" + \" \".join(f\"{x:.3f}\" for x in row2))\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6565,"cache_read_input_tokens":158103,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6565},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0d212505-2370-4998-83f2-5c6798c04a82","uuid":"efbf3440-f384-4eb4-bdf4-e9c5eaec227d","timestamp":"2026-11-06T23:38:09.802Z","request_id":"req_011CgnZ7j2FVQvPZ3ME2E15J"}
